00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:03.660
YUFENG GUO: Don't you just
love setting up your computer,

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:06.000
installing libraries,
configuring them, and making

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.360
sure they're all compatible?

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:09.610
It's just so much fun.

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:10.300
No?

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:10.800
Yeah.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:12.300
Me neither.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.870
That's why we're going to skip
around all that nonsense today

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:19.650
using Deep Learning VM Images
on Google Compute Engine.

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:31.380
Welcome to AI Adventures where
we explore the art, science,

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:33.630
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:36.000
My name is Yufeng Guo,
and on this episode,

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.990
we're going to check out how to
take advantage of Deep Learning

00:00:38.990 --> 00:00:42.740
VM Images to make setting
up a new environment a piece

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:44.110
of cake.

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:46.160
The Google Cloud Deep
Learning VM Image

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:48.680
makes it easy and
fast to instantiate

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:51.620
a VM image containing the
most popular deep learning

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:55.140
and machine learning frameworks,
all on a Google Compute Engine

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:55.860
instance.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:59.540
It lets you launch Compute
Engine instances preinstalled

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:01.370
with your choice
of ML frameworks

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:04.010
like TensorFlow or even PyTorch.

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:09.380
And you can easily add Cloud
TPU and, of course, GPU support.

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:10.880
You can either
instantiate the image

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:14.060
using the Google Cloud
Platform Cloud Launcher UI

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:16.470
or from the command line.

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:19.160
So now you get to use
your favorite open source

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:23.180
tools even sooner without having
to keep them up to date or deal

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:25.100
with installation troubles.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:27.590
The Deep Learning VM
Images are prepackaged

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:30.440
as a click-to-deploy
virtual machine that

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:32.360
can be accessed in
the marketplace,

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:34.490
formerly known as
Cloud Launcher.

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:37.730
Search for Deep
Learning VM and select

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:39.140
Launch on Compute Engine.

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:42.290
There, you can select from
a number of different Deep

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:44.510
Learning VM Images
with configurations

00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:48.680
of CPU, memory, storage,
GPU, and your choice

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:50.180
of installed libraries.

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:52.610
There are just a ton of
combinations available,

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:56.450
and you can fine tune them
for your specific workload.

00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:59.000
The best part is that
unlike a typical computer

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.490
under your desk, the
hardware for a VM

00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:04.220
can be edited at
a moment's notice.

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:06.500
Need more memory,
double the disk space,

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:09.470
or say another GPU or four?

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:11.310
Just edit away.

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:14.510
Let's take a look at how you
can create these VMs using

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:16.460
the gcloud command line tool.

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:19.370
We will start with the simpler
case of a virtual machine

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:22.130
with only CPU, no GPU.

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:24.290
In this case, we
just need to decide

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:27.680
what zone to put our VM in
and what machine learning

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:30.150
library we want to use with it.

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:32.450
Here's the list of all
the available images

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:33.570
to choose from.

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:37.970
Let's say we do tf-latest-cpu.

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:41.790
The full command to run this
word look something like this.

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:43.970
And once you run this,
it will take a moment

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:46.250
to provision the machine
and install all the software

00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:47.690
packages.

00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:51.740
Once the VM is up and running,
you can SSH into the machine.

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:53.660
Instead of a
standard SSH command,

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:56.480
I would recommend using
this slightly modified SSH

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:58.760
command, which does a
bit of port forwarding

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:01.940
for you to enable you
to connect to JupyterLab

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:05.240
via your local Chrome browser,
just like you were running it

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:06.980
locally.

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:08.560
Now, when you run
this command, it

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:10.690
will drop you into
an SSH session.

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:13.190
You can poke around there in
the command line if you'd like,

00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:16.640
but the real good stuff
awaits in your browser.

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:20.660
Head over to localhost-8080,
and it'll magically

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:21.830
load up JupyterLab.

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:23.240
It'll just be there.

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:27.080
Now we can do a whole separate
video on just JupyterLab alone

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:31.160
but, in brief, it's like Jupyter
Notebooks but way cooler.

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:34.190
You can manage your entire data
science workflow from here,

00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:36.680
running multiple notebooks
in different tabs

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:38.840
and with different
versions of Python even

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:41.771
and take some notes,
say, in a text file.

00:03:41.771 --> 00:03:42.270
OK.

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:43.820
So that's the
end-to-end workflow

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:47.370
for a CPU-powered
virtual machine.

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:51.080
Now let's say we want GPUs,
because it seems like everyone

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:52.910
wants GPUs these days.

00:03:52.910 --> 00:03:54.620
There are a few
additional considerations

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:56.480
here that you'll need
to keep in mind as you

00:03:56.480 --> 00:03:58.160
work through this process.

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:00.380
The tricky part of
doing all of this

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:03.350
is that you need to coordinate
just a couple of things

00:04:03.350 --> 00:04:05.420
to get these GPUs working right.

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:08.090
First, let's take a look
at what GPUs are available.

00:04:08.090 --> 00:04:10.630
As of this recording,
there are options

00:04:10.630 --> 00:04:15.290
that include the K80,
the P100, and the V100.

00:04:15.290 --> 00:04:17.690
So you'll need to perhaps
do a little bit of research

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:20.579
to choose which one
you want to use.

00:04:20.579 --> 00:04:23.240
Once you've decided
on your GPU of choice,

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:26.690
check out what regions a
given GPU is available.

00:04:26.690 --> 00:04:29.960
You can see that shown here.

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:33.680
And finally, head over to your
Quotas page in Cloud Console

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:35.720
to ensure that you
have enough quota

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:39.500
to provision a machine with
that combination of GPU

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:41.000
in that zone.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:44.660
This final step is crucial to
getting your VM provisioned

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:45.620
successfully.

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:47.780
If you don't have
the necessary quota,

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:49.640
go ahead and click
on Edit Quotas

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:51.110
and submit a quota request.

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:53.780
On the surface, it seems
to be all about having

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:55.040
the necessary quota.

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:58.640
But it's a bit more nuanced than
that since the quota specifies

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:00.800
not only what model
of GPU but also

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:03.180
what region that GPU sits in.

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:03.680
OK.

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:05.870
So say you've figured
out what GPU you want,

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:07.580
what zone it should
be in, and confirmed

00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:10.430
that you have the necessary
quota to provision it.

00:05:10.430 --> 00:05:13.970
Now let's look at how to
actually get a GPU-powered Deep

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:15.980
Learning VM going.

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:19.040
The step that differs from
all the previous CPU workflow

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:22.370
that we just saw is how we
actually start up the VM.

00:05:22.370 --> 00:05:25.880
We can take the command that
we used before and add to it

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:27.960
a few extra bargains.

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:29.760
First, we'll need to
set the maintenance

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:31.800
policy to terminate.

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:34.230
This is due to the fact
that we can't live migrate

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:36.130
a GPU-powered virtual machine.

00:05:36.130 --> 00:05:38.340
So for maintenance, it'll
actually shut down the VM,

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:40.050
but you can have
it auto restart.

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:43.000
So always be sure
to save your work.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:47.550
Next, choose your GPU type and
count, informed by that quota

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:49.450
deep dive we just did.

00:05:49.450 --> 00:05:52.140
And finally add a
piece of metadata,

00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:55.830
which will ensure that the
drivers to power your GPU

00:05:55.830 --> 00:05:58.506
are installed on your behalf.

00:05:58.506 --> 00:05:59.880
It's a couple of
additional steps

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:01.970
to get these
GPU-powered VMs working,

00:06:01.970 --> 00:06:04.380
but I promise it's
worth the effort.

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:06.780
Once you run this command,
the rest of the workflow

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:08.260
is exactly the same.

00:06:08.260 --> 00:06:10.290
And when you SSH
into your machine,

00:06:10.290 --> 00:06:12.250
JupyterLab is there
waiting for you.

00:06:12.250 --> 00:06:13.830
And if you do a
PIP freeze, you can

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:16.320
see that the version of
TensorFlow that is installed

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:17.460
is tensorflow-gpu.

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:22.650
So the next time you're
thinking about installing

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:25.310
a bunch of libraries
by hand, remember

00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:27.510
to save yourself
some time and energy

00:06:27.510 --> 00:06:30.720
by using the Deep Learning
VM Images on Google Cloud

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:31.650
Platform.

